  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Finnida From the Ground  -Oxfam Africa</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam in Africa value add for this project is to facilitate cohesive cross-country CSO collaboration and to lead on regional and international tax advocacy issues will lead the advocacy and influencing work on this project .More specifically, to inform the development of regional advocacy strategies, we will undertake two pieces of research; an analysis and synthesis of the FTM reports from Kenya and Mozambique country teams to develop a regional policy proposal, and the second piece will be focused on Extractives pegged on what the Kenya, Mozambique and South Africa are focused on.

After each piece has produced, a convening of CSOs will follow to develop a advocacy strategy that target the Regional Economic Communities (EAC, ECOWAS and SADC), the African Union and its relevant institutions, and International spaces/fora. The objective is to build voice and consensus of African stakeholder to effectively engage in efforts to end extractive sector related tax avoidance and illicit financial flows. Through our AU liaison office, we will also be supporting country teams and partners to effectively influence the regional frameworks on African tax and extractives agenda, as well as international frameworks on tax and extractives. The objective is to strengthen Africa&#x2019;s agency in global discourse/conversation on tax and extractives. Also, when taking part in &#x2018;global north&#x2019; and &#x2018;global south; coordination efforts related to this project, Oxfam in Africa will lead the convening the global south countries and partners.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org ref="NL-KVK-27108436" role="4" type="21">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam International was formed in 1995 by a group of independent non-governmental organizations. Their aim was to work together forgreater impact on the international stage to reduce poverty and injustice.
The name &quot;Oxfam&quot; comes from the Oxford Committee for Famine Relief, founded in Britain in 1942. The group campaigned for food supplies to be sent through an allied naval blockade to starving women and children in enemy-occupied Greece during the Second World War.
As well as becoming a world leader in the delivery of emergency relief, Oxfam International implements long-term development programs in vulnerable communities. We are also part of a global movement, campaigning with others, for instance, to end unfair trade rules, demand better health and education services for all, and to combat climate change.
Today, there are 19 member organizations of the Oxfam International confederation. They are based in: Australia, Belgium, Canada, Denmark, France, Germany, Great Britain, Hong Kong, Ireland, India, Italy, Japan, Mexico, The Netherlands, New Zealand, Quebec, South Africa, Spain and the United States.
The Oxfam International Secretariat is based in Oxford, UK. The Secretariat runs advocacy offices in Addis Ababa, Brussels, Geneva, New York and Washington DC.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam Novib</narrative>
